Observer Design Pattern — Video Upload Notification Example
The Observer Design Pattern is a behavioral design pattern in which one object
notifies multiple dependent objects automatically whenever its state changes.

Let's understand it using a simple example: a video platform where subscribers receive
notifications whenever a creator uploads a new video.


1. Subscriber.java
Role: Observer interface.
Declares the update() method.
Every subscriber must implement this method to receive notifications.

2. UserSubscriber.java
Role: Concrete Observer.
Represents an individual user subscribed to a channel.
Implements update() to display video upload notifications.

3. Channel.java
Role: Subject / Publisher.
Maintains a list of subscribers.
subscribe() adds a subscriber.
unsubscribe() removes a subscriber.
uploadVideo() simulates a video upload.
notifySubscribers() calls update() on every registered subscriber.

4. Main.java
Role: Application entry point and demo.
Creates the channel and subscriber objects.
Registers subscribers, simulates uploads, and demonstrates unsubscribing.
Key Concept

The Channel does not depend on individual subscriber implementations. It depends on the
Subscriber interface, making the system loosely coupled.

When the channel's state changes (a video is uploaded), all registered observers are notified
automatically.